# The clause-by-clause conformance corpus for the Classic Refal-5 builtin
# library.
#
# Every row has four `|`-separated fields, and its first field says what the row
# is:
#
#   clause|CLAUSE|SUBJECT|MODE
#       binds a clause of the reference to what exercises it. MODE is `run`,
#       meaning SUBJECT is a fixture with a `run` row below; `fail`, meaning it
#       is a fixture with a `fail` row; or `unit`, meaning SUBJECT names a test
#       function instead -- which is how the clauses that need a filesystem path
#       or an environment the CLI cannot give a committed program are covered.
#   run|FIXTURE|EXPECTED|ARGS
#       FIXTURE prints exactly EXPECTED, with `\n` for a newline, when it is run
#       with ARGS (space-separated, possibly empty) and its standard input
#       closed.
#   fail|FIXTURE|DIAGNOSTIC|
#       FIXTURE is accepted by `check` and fails when it runs, with DIAGNOSTIC
#       somewhere in the message.
#
# The reference is http://www.refal.net/refer_r5.html: C.1 input/output, C.2
# arithmetic, C.3 the buried-data stack, C.4 characters and strings, C.5 the
# system functions -- and section D for the tracer's rendering of an unknown,
# which is what section 6.4 of Chapter 6 defines the unknown for.
#
# `every_builtin_clause_has_a_traceable_fixture` reads this file, requires the
# clause set below to match the clauses the reference defines, requires every
# fixture and every named test to exist, and runs every row.

# --- what each clause is exercised by
clause|c1.1|examples/builtin-io-conformance.ref|run
clause|c1.2|examples/builtin-io-conformance.ref|run
clause|c1.3|examples/builtin-io-conformance.ref|run
clause|c1.4|reads_and_writes_descriptor_backed_files|unit
clause|c1.5|examples/builtin-io-conformance.ref|run
clause|c1.5|reads_and_writes_descriptor_backed_files|unit
clause|c1.6|reads_and_writes_descriptor_backed_files|unit
clause|c1.7|reads_and_writes_descriptor_backed_files|unit
clause|c1.8|examples/builtin-io-conformance.ref|run

